-- title:
--   Finitely many affinoid points miss a given generic neighbourhood
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $r$ and a ring of coefficients: a characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O$ which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), an irreducible element $\pi$ (`hπ`), with $\mathcal O$ complete for the $\pi$-adic topology (`hcomplete`), residue ring of cardinality $r$ (`hres`) and $(r)=(\pi)$ as ideals of $\mathcal O$ (`hunr`); $K_0$ is a characteristic-zero field which is the fraction field of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   Unramified coefficients with Frobenius: $Onr$ is a characteristic-zero domain and $\mathcal O$-algebra with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $Fr$, such that $Onr$ is complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`), that ideal is maximal (`hOnr_max`), every $x\in Onr$ satisfies some monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial over $Onr$ of positive degree has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`), and $Fr(x)\equiv x^{r}\pmod{\pi}$ for all $x$ (`hFr`). Further, $vdet:\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)\to\mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) is a homomorphism with $vdet(g)=n$ if and only if $\det g=u\pi^{n}$ in $K_0$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal O$ (`hvdet`).
--
--   Group data: a group $G$, a homomorphism $\sigma:G\to\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ and a subgroup $\Gamma\le G$ such that some $z\in\Gamma$ has $\sigma z$ a scalar matrix with $vdet(\sigma z)=2$ (`hcent`) and some $w\in\Gamma$ has $vdet(\sigma w)=1$ (`hodd`); $\Gamma'$ is a subgroup whose elements are exactly the $x\in\Gamma$ with $vdet(\sigma x)$ even (`hΓ'`). The homomorphism $\rho:G\to \mathrm{PGL}_2(K_0)$ is the projectivisation of $\sigma$ (`hρ`), and on the vertex set of the lattice tree of $\mathcal O$ in $K_0$ (homothety classes of full lattices in $K_0^2$) the image $\rho(\Gamma')$ acts with finite vertex stabilisers (`hdisc`) and with finitely many orbits, witnessed by a finite set $S$ of vertices meeting every orbit (`hcocpt`).
--
--   Geometric data: a scheme $\mathcal X$ with a proper flat morphism $f:\mathcal X\to\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ whose base change to $K_0$, namely the second projection of the pullback of $f$ along $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\mathcal O\to K_0$, is smooth of relative dimension $1$ (`hsmooth`).
--
--   Uniformisation of infinitesimal points: $\Theta$ assigns, to each $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which the image of $\pi$ is nilpotent, a map from pairs $(\psi,P)$ — $\psi$ an $\mathcal O$-algebra homomorphism $Onr\to B$ and $P$ a Deligne datum over $B$ in the sense of `Omega K₀ π`, i.e. a family of $B$-submodules of the base changes of full lattices, with invertible quotients, compatible with lattice inclusions, equivariant for homotheties, and nondegenerate at every prime of $B$ — to morphisms $\operatorname{Spec} B\to\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$. It is natural in $B$ (`hΘnat`); it is invariant under the twisted action `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct`, which relates $x$ and $x'$ when the first component of $x'$ is the first component of $x$ precomposed with $Fr^{-vdet(g)}$ and the Deligne datum of $x'$ is the pullback of that of $x$ along $g^{-1}$, for $g=\sigma\gamma$ with $\gamma\in\Gamma$ (`hΘinv`); over every algebraically closed field $k$ with $\pi$ nilpotent and every $\psi:Onr\to k$, the maps $\Theta(\psi,-)$ are surjective onto the $k$-points of $f$ and have fibres exactly the twisted $\Gamma$-orbits (`hΘfib`); and $\Theta$ is universal among such data: for every $t:T\to\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and every natural, twisted-invariant family $\rho'$ there is a natural family $u$ of maps on nilpotent points with $u\circ\Theta=\rho'$, and any natural $u'$ with $u'\circ\Theta=\rho'$ agrees with $u$ (`hΘuniv`).
--
--   Analytic frame: $C$ is a complete, algebraically closed valued field with value group in $\Gamma_0$ and a $K_0$-algebra structure; $R$ is a commutative $\mathcal O$-algebra with $C$ an $R$-algebra, compatibly with $\mathcal O$ and $K_0$. The element $\varpi$ is a pseudo-uniformiser of $K_0$ in $C$: $0<v(\varpi)<1$ in $C$ and for every $a\in K_0^\times$ some power $v(\varpi)^N$ bounds $v(a)$ below while $v(\varpi)^{-N}$ bounds it above. The hypothesis `hF` asserts that $(\pi,\varpi,R)$ is an adic frame: $\pi$ is irreducible, $R\to C$ is injective with image the set $\{v\le 1\}$, $R$ is $\pi$-adically complete, the elements of $K_0$ of valuation $\le 1$ in $C$ are exactly those of $\mathcal O$, the image of $K_0$ in $C$ is closed, and $\pi$ and $\varpi$ have the same image in $C$. Moreover $\psi_0:Onr\to R$ is an $\mathcal O$-algebra homomorphism; `hrk` asserts that for $x,y\in C$ with $v(x)<1$ and $y\ne 0$ some power $v(x)^n$ is $\le v(y)$; `hval` asserts that every nonzero $\varepsilon\in\Gamma_0$ dominates $v(y)$ for some $y\ne0$ in $C$; `hex` asserts that the affinoids $\mathrm{affinoid}\,\varpi\,n=\{z\in C: v(z)\le v(\varpi)^{-n}$ and $v(\varpi)^{n}\le v(z-a)$ for all $a\in K_0$ with $v(a)\le v(\varpi)^{-n}\}$ exhaust the Drinfeld upper half-plane $C\setminus K_0$; and the ring `Omega.HolRingOf ϖ ρ` of functions holomorphic on every affinoid is assumed to be a domain.
--
--   Uniformisation of adic points: $\Phi$ sends each adic point $x$ of `AdicPoint K₀ π R` — a system of Deligne data over the quotients $R/(\pi^{n+1})$, compatible under the transition maps — to an $R$-point of $\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, i.e. a morphism $p:\operatorname{Spec} R\to\mathcal X$ with $p$ followed by $f$ equal to the structure morphism of $R$. The hypothesis `hΦ` has four conjuncts: for all $x$ and $n$, reducing $\Phi x$ modulo $\pi^{n+1}$ gives $\Theta$ applied to the pair consisting of $\psi_0$ followed by the quotient map and the $n$-th Deligne datum of $x$; $\Phi$ is surjective; $\Phi x=\Phi x'$ holds if and only if $x'=x.\mathrm{act}(\sigma\gamma)$ for some $\gamma\in\Gamma'$; and base change along $R\to C$ is injective on these $R$-points and every morphism $\operatorname{Spec} C\to\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ arises from one of them.
--
--   Finally $s_C:\operatorname{Spec} C\to\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ is the morphism induced by $\mathcal O\to K_0\to C$ (`hsC`), and the pullback $\mathcal X_C:=\mathcal X\times_{\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O}\operatorname{Spec} C$ is assumed integral.
--
--   Under these hypotheses the conclusion is: for every open subset $U$ of $\mathcal X_C$ containing the generic point of $\mathcal X_C$ and every $n\in\mathbb N$, the set of points $z$ of the affinoid $\mathrm{affinoid}\,\varpi\,n$ for which there exists an adic point $x$ with $z=x.\mathrm{toOmega}\,C$ (the unique $z\in C$ with $(z,1)$ on the $C$-line of $x$, and $0$ if no such $z$ is unique) and there exists a morphism $q:\operatorname{Spec} C\to\mathcal X_C$ such that $q$ followed by the first projection is the base change along $R\to C$ of $\Phi x$, $q$ followed by the second projection is the identity of $\operatorname{Spec} C$, and the preimage $q^{-1}U$ is not all of $\operatorname{Spec} C$, is finite.
--
--   This is the finiteness step in the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation of the curve $\mathcal X_C$: on each affinoid of the Drinfeld upper half-plane, only finitely many uniformised points can fail to land in a prescribed open neighbourhood of the generic point of $\mathcal X_C$. It is the version of the statement that takes smoothness of relative dimension one of the generic fibre as a hypothesis, and it feeds the construction of $\Gamma'$-invariant chartwise meromorphic functions and of the resulting embedding of the function field of $\mathcal X_C$ into the invariant holomorphic functions on the half-plane.
